# Long-case expansion L4 (target ~30 Qs): alopecia areata, acne vulgaris, androgenetic alopecia, BCC, SCC.
# Own words; viva-length answers. Facts from IADVL 5e and current guidelines.
D = [
dict(case="Alopecia areata", qa=[
 ("What is immune privilege of the hair follicle and its collapse?", ["Anagen bulb normally has low MHC class I, local immunosuppressants (TGF-β, α-MSH, IL-10, CGRP) — protecting follicle autoantigens", "In AA, IFN-γ (and stressors/ viral triggers) upregulates MHC class I/II, NKG2D ligands (MICA, ULBP3) → CD8+ NKG2D+ T cells attack the bulb → dystrophic anagen/ catagen; IFN-γ–JAK1/2 and IL-15–JAK1/3 signalling (rationale for JAK inhibitors)"]),
 ("Describe the exclamation-mark hair and other signs.", ["Exclamation-mark hair: short broken hair, thicker distally and tapering proximally with depigmented root — sign of active disease (at the edge of patches)", "Positive hair-pull test at margin (active), Coudability sign (hairs bend at the base), cadaver hairs/ black dots, regrowth of white (vellus) hairs first", "Sudden whitening ('turning white overnight') — selective loss of pigmented hairs"]),
 ("Describe the clinical patterns.", ["Patchy (commonest), multifocal, reticular, ophiasis (band along occipital and temporal margins — poor prognosis), sisaipho (ophiasis inversus — frontoparietal), diffuse AA (mimics TE), alopecia totalis (whole scalp), universalis (all body hair), AA incognita (diffuse thinning with yellow dots), beard/ eyebrow/ eyelash AA"]),
 ("Associated conditions.", ["Atopy (eczema, asthma — poorer prognosis), thyroid autoimmunity, vitiligo, type 1 diabetes, coeliac disease, lupus, Down syndrome (higher prevalence), autoimmune polyglandular syndrome type 1 (APECED), psychiatric stress, iron/ vitamin D deficiency"]),
 ("Nail changes in AA.", ["Fine regular geometric pitting ('Scotch plaid'), trachyonychia, longitudinal ridging, red/ mottled lunulae, onychomadesis — more common in severe AA (marker of poor prognosis)"]),
 ("How is SALT score calculated?", ["Severity of Alopecia Tool: scalp divided — vertex 40%, right side 18%, left side 18%, posterior 24%; % hair loss in each area × its weight summed (0–100); S1 25–49%, S2 25–49 ... (S0 no loss, S1 <25%, S2 25–49%, S3 50–74%, S4a 75–95%, S4b 96–99%, S5 100%); SALT ≤20 used as response target in trials"]),
 ("Intralesional steroid therapy — practical points.", ["Triamcinolone acetonide 2.5–10 mg/ml (5 mg/ml commonly on scalp, 2.5 mg/ml on face/eyebrows), 0.05–0.1 ml per injection point 1 cm apart into deep dermis, every 4–6 weeks; maximum ~20 mg per session; side effects — atrophy (reversible), hypopigmentation, telangiectasia; best for limited patchy AA (<50% scalp) in adults"]),
 ("Topical immunotherapy — mechanism and protocol.", ["Induces allergic contact dermatitis with diphenylcyclopropenone (DPCP) or squaric acid dibutyl ester (SADBE) — antigenic competition shifts the immune response away from the follicle ('perifollicular CD4/CD8 ratio change')", "Sensitise with 2% on a small area, then weekly applications starting 0.001% and increasing until mild eczema persists 24–36 h; continue 12–24 weeks; response ~50–60% in extensive AA; side effects — severe eczema, lymphadenopathy, pigment changes (vitiligo/ dyschromia in confetti), urticaria"]),
 ("Systemic steroids in AA — regimens.", ["Oral mini-pulse: betamethasone/dexamethasone 5 mg on two consecutive days weekly for 3–6 months", "Monthly pulses (prednisolone 300 mg or methylprednisolone 500 mg × 3 days), short courses of prednisolone 0.5–1 mg/kg tapering — relapse common on stopping; use for rapidly progressive disease"]),
 ("JAK inhibitors in AA — details.", ["Baricitinib (JAK1/2) 2–4 mg/day — approved for severe AA (≥50% SALT) in adults; ritlecitinib (JAK3/ TEC) 50 mg/day — approved ≥12 y; deuruxolitinib (JAK1/2) approved in US; tofacitinib off-label (cheaper — widely used in India)", "Response: SALT ≤20 in ~35–40% at 36 weeks, better with longer duration; eyebrow/eyelash regrowth; relapse after stopping", "Monitoring: TB, hepatitis, CBC, lipids, LFT, CK; risks — infections, acne, VTE, MACE in older smokers"]),
 ("Other treatments.", ["Topical minoxidil 5% (adjunct), anthralin short-contact (irritant therapy), topical steroids (children), methotrexate (± steroids), cyclosporine, azathioprine, simvastatin/ezetimibe (weak), excimer laser, PRP, low-level laser; hairpieces/ wigs, eyebrow tattooing/ microblading, psychological support", "Children: topical steroids, minoxidil, anthralin, DPCP (older children), mini-pulse steroids for extensive progressive disease"]),
 ("Poor prognostic factors in AA.", ["Onset in childhood, long duration (>1 year), extensive loss (totalis/universalis), ophiasis pattern, nail involvement, atopy, family history, associated autoimmunity, Down syndrome, multiple relapses"]),
 ("Differentiate AA from tinea capitis and trichotillomania.", ["Tinea capitis: scaling, inflammation, broken hairs, black dots, comma/corkscrew hairs on trichoscopy, KOH positive", "Trichotillomania: irregular patches with hairs of varying lengths, no exclamation-mark hairs, broken hairs with trichoptilosis, V-sign, flame hairs on trichoscopy, behavioural history", "AA: smooth patch, exclamation-mark hairs, yellow dots, regrowing vellus hairs"]),
 ("What psychiatric issues are linked and how to address?", ["Anxiety, depression, social phobia, low self-esteem; validated QoL tools (AA-QLI, Skindex); offer counselling, support groups, camouflage; screen children for bullying"]),
 ("What is the role of vitamin D and iron in AA?", ["Low serum vitamin D and ferritin are frequently reported in AA; correct deficiencies (not proven as primary treatment); topical calcipotriol has modest benefit in patchy AA"]),
 ("Histopathology of AA by stage.", ["Acute: peribulbar 'swarm of bees' lymphocytic infiltrate around anagen bulbs, eosinophils, pigment incontinence", "Subacute: increased catagen/telogen hairs", "Chronic: miniaturised follicles, fibrous streamers, reduced terminal:vellus ratio, minimal inflammation; no scarring — follicles preserved"]),
 ("Differential diagnosis of diffuse AA.", ["Telogen effluvium (normal trichoscopy, club hairs), androgenetic alopecia (miniaturisation pattern, no exclamation hairs), anagen effluvium (chemotherapy), syphilitic alopecia (moth-eaten), SLE alopecia, loose anagen syndrome (children — ruffled cuticle, painless pluckable hairs)"]),
 ("Prognosis in patchy AA.", ["Most limited patchy AA regrow within 1 year (spontaneous regrowth in ~50%); relapses common (~85% have more than one episode); ~5–10% progress to totalis/universalis"]),
 ]),
dict(case="Acne vulgaris", qa=[
 ("What is the role of Cutibacterium acnes?", ["Anaerobic commensal of sebaceous follicles; certain phylotypes (IA1) associated with acne; activates TLR2/4 on keratinocytes and monocytes, inflammasome (NLRP3) → IL-1β, IL-8; forms biofilms; lipases release free fatty acids; target for benzoyl peroxide and antibiotics"]),
 ("Explain hormonal influences.", ["Androgens (DHT, DHEA-S, testosterone) enlarge sebaceous glands and increase sebum; IGF-1 (high glycaemic diets, dairy, puberty) enhances androgen effects and lipogenesis via mTORC1; menstrual flares (progesterone), PCOS, congenital adrenal hyperplasia, androgen-secreting tumours, insulin resistance"]),
 ("Describe the role of diet in acne.", ["Moderate evidence: high-glycaemic-load diets and skimmed milk/whey protein supplements aggravate acne (insulin/IGF-1–mTORC1); chocolate/ fatty foods — weak evidence; advise low-glycaemic balanced diet, avoid whey protein supplements"]),
 ("What is the global acne grading system (GAGS) and other grading?", ["GAGS: six regions with factors (forehead 2, each cheek 2, nose 1, chin 1, chest+upper back 3) × lesion grade (0 none, 1 comedone, 2 papule, 3 pustule, 4 nodule); total 1–18 mild, 19–30 moderate, 31–38 severe, ≥39 very severe", "Indian (IADVL) grading: Grade 1 comedones ± few papules; Grade 2 papules, comedones, few pustules; Grade 3 predominant pustules, nodules, abscesses; Grade 4 mainly cysts, abscesses, widespread scarring"]),
 ("Describe acne fulminans and its management.", ["Severe, sudden ulcerative haemorrhagic nodular acne on trunk/face in adolescent boys, often triggered by isotretinoin start or testosterone; systemic — fever, arthralgia, myalgia, osteolytic bone lesions (sternum, clavicles), hepatosplenomegaly, raised ESR/ leucocytosis", "Treatment: prednisolone 0.5–1 mg/kg for 2–4 weeks then add low-dose isotretinoin (0.1 mg/kg) slowly while tapering steroids; dapsone; avoid starting full-dose isotretinoin"]),
 ("What is the SAPHO and PAPA syndromes?", ["SAPHO: Synovitis, Acne (conglobata/fulminans), Pustulosis (palmoplantar), Hyperostosis, Osteitis (anterior chest wall)", "PAPA: Pyogenic Arthritis, Pyoderma gangrenosum, Acne — PSTPIP1 autoinflammatory; PASH (PG, acne, hidradenitis), PAPASH; treat with IL-1/ TNF blockade"]),
 ("Antibiotic stewardship in acne.", ["Never use topical or oral antibiotics as monotherapy — combine with benzoyl peroxide (prevents resistance) and a retinoid; limit oral antibiotics to 3–4 months; avoid using different topical and oral antibiotics together; prefer doxycycline/minocycline (sarecycline — narrow spectrum); macrolides only when tetracyclines contraindicated"]),
 ("Hormonal therapy in women.", ["Combined oral contraceptives (ethinyl oestradiol with anti-androgenic progestin — cyproterone acetate, drospirenone, desogestrel) — 3–6 months for benefit; contraindications (thrombosis, migraine with aura, smoking >35 y)", "Spironolactone 50–150 mg/day (anti-androgen) — hyperkalaemia rare in healthy women, menstrual irregularity, breast tenderness; avoid in pregnancy (feminisation of male fetus)", "Topical clascoterone 1% (androgen receptor antagonist) for ≥12 y"]),
 ("Isotretinoin — dosing strategies and newer evidence.", ["0.5–1 mg/kg/day to a cumulative dose of 120–150 mg/kg (reduces relapse); low-dose (0.25–0.4 mg/kg or 10–20 mg/day) for moderate acne — similar efficacy with fewer side effects, longer duration", "Monitoring: pregnancy prevention (2 contraceptive methods, tests monthly, avoid pregnancy 1 month after), baseline and 1–2 month lipid profile and LFT; depression/ suicide monitoring (causality unproven), IBD (no causal link), avoid tetracyclines (pseudotumour cerebri), avoid waxing/ laser resurfacing during and 6 months after (old advice now relaxed)"]),
 ("Topical retinoids — mechanism and pearls.", ["Bind RAR-γ/β → normalise follicular keratinisation (comedolytic), anti-inflammatory (TLR2 down-regulation), improve post-inflammatory pigmentation; tretinoin 0.025–0.1%, adapalene 0.1–0.3% (most stable, least irritating, OTC), tazarotene 0.1% (pregnancy category X), trifarotene 0.005% (RAR-γ selective — trunk acne)", "Apply thin layer at night to whole affected area, start alternate nights, sunscreen; retinoid dermatitis — irritation, dryness; first-line maintenance therapy"]),
 ("Benzoyl peroxide and azelaic acid.", ["BPO 2.5–5%: oxidising agent — bactericidal (no resistance), mildly comedolytic; bleaches fabrics, irritation; combine with clindamycin/ adapalene", "Azelaic acid 15–20%: antibacterial, comedolytic, anti-inflammatory, tyrosinase inhibition — good for PIH-prone darker skin, pregnancy-safe"]),
 ("Differentiate acne from rosacea and folliculitis.", ["Acne: comedones (hallmark), adolescents, face/back/chest, nodules/scars", "Rosacea: no comedones, central face, flushing, telangiectasia, adults, sensitive skin", "Folliculitis: monomorphic pustules around hairs, no comedones, may be bacterial/ Malassezia/ Gram-negative"]),
 ("What are the types of acne scars and their treatments?", ["Atrophic: ice-pick (narrow deep — TCA CROSS, punch excision), rolling (broad, tethered — subcision, fillers, microneedling), boxcar (sharp edges — punch elevation, fractional lasers, chemical peels)", "Hypertrophic/ keloidal: intralesional steroids, silicone, PDL, cryotherapy", "Combined approach: fractional CO2/ Er:YAG, microneedling ± PRP, radiofrequency microneedling, fillers; treat active acne first"]),
 ("What is post-inflammatory hyperpigmentation in acne and its management?", ["Common in Indian skin; prevent by early treatment of inflammatory acne and sunscreen; treat with azelaic acid, retinoids, niacinamide, hydroquinone short-term, chemical peels (glycolic/ salicylic), avoid picking"]),
 ("Acne in pregnancy — safe treatments.", ["Topical azelaic acid, benzoyl peroxide (limited area), topical clindamycin/ erythromycin; oral erythromycin/ azithromycin if needed; avoid retinoids (topical and oral), tetracyclines, spironolactone, hormonal therapy"]),
 ("What is drug-induced acne and acneiform eruption?", ["Monomorphic papulopustules without comedones on trunk: corticosteroids, anabolic steroids/ testosterone, lithium, isoniazid, phenytoin, halogens (iodide, bromide), vitamin B6/B12, cyclosporine, EGFR inhibitors (papulopustular rash — treat with doxycycline, topical steroids), JAK inhibitors, dupilumab (facial), progesterone-only contraceptives"]),
 ("What is acne mechanica and acne cosmetica?", ["Acne mechanica: friction/ occlusion/ pressure (helmets, straps, backpacks, sports gear) — avoid trigger", "Acne cosmetica: comedones from occlusive cosmetics/ hair oils (pomade acne on forehead) — use 'non-comedogenic' oil-free products"]),
 ("What investigations are needed in a woman with acne and hirsutism?", ["Menstrual history, BMI, signs of virilisation; early follicular phase total/ free testosterone, DHEA-S, 17-OH progesterone (CAH), LH:FSH, prolactin, TSH, fasting glucose/insulin; pelvic ultrasound (PCOS — Rotterdam criteria 2 of 3: oligo/anovulation, hyperandrogenism, polycystic ovaries)"]),
 ]),
dict(case="Androgenetic alopecia", qa=[
 ("Describe the Hamilton–Norwood classification.", ["Male pattern: I — minimal frontotemporal recession; II — symmetrical triangular frontotemporal recession; III — deep recession (III vertex — vertex thinning with stage II recession); IV — further recession + vertex balding separated by band; V — band narrower; VI — bridge lost, confluent; VII — only horseshoe band at sides and back; type A variants — anterior-to-posterior progression without vertex"]),
 ("Describe the Ludwig and Sinclair scales for female pattern hair loss.", ["Ludwig: grade I — mild crown thinning with preserved frontal hairline; II — marked; III — near-total crown baldness", "Sinclair: 1–5 based on widening of the central part (stage 1 normal → 5 advanced); Olsen 'Christmas tree' pattern — frontal accentuation of part widening"]),
 ("Explain the pathogenesis in detail.", ["Genetic (polygenic — androgen receptor gene on X chromosome, EDA2R, 20p11) + androgens: testosterone converted to DHT by 5α-reductase type 2 (and 1) in dermal papilla → DHT binds AR → shortening of anagen, progressive follicular miniaturisation (terminal → vellus-like), increased telogen", "Other factors: perifollicular micro-inflammation and fibrosis, Wnt/β-catenin down-regulation, PGD2 (inhibits hair growth) increase, ageing, oxidative stress"]),
 ("Trichoscopy features.", ["Hair shaft thickness variability >20% (anisotrichosis), increased vellus hairs, yellow dots (few), perifollicular brown halo (peripilar sign), single-hair follicular units, scalp pigmentation honeycomb; frontal more than occipital differences"]),
 ("Minoxidil — mechanism, use and side effects.", ["Potassium-channel opener → vasodilation, prolongs anagen, enlarges miniaturised follicles, VEGF up-regulation; topical 5% (men; 2–5% women) twice daily or foam; effect after 3–6 months, maintained only while used", "Low-dose oral minoxidil 0.25–5 mg/day — increasingly used (better adherence); side effects — hypertrichosis, pedal oedema, tachycardia, postural hypotension; topical — irritant/allergic dermatitis (propylene glycol), initial shedding, facial hypertrichosis", "Sulfotransferase activity in follicle predicts response"]),
 ("Finasteride and dutasteride.", ["Finasteride 1 mg/day (type 2 5α-reductase inhibitor — reduces scalp DHT ~60%); dutasteride 0.5 mg (types 1 and 2, more potent); effect after 6–12 months; side effects — reduced libido, erectile dysfunction (~1–2%), gynaecomastia, depression ('post-finasteride syndrome' debated); lowers PSA by ~50% (double the value for screening); teratogenic — women of child-bearing potential shouldn't handle crushed tablets", "Topical finasteride 0.25% spray — lower systemic levels"]),
 ("Treatment of female pattern hair loss.", ["Topical minoxidil 2–5%, low-dose oral minoxidil, anti-androgens (spironolactone 50–200 mg/day, cyproterone acetate with OCP, flutamide/ bicalutamide), finasteride/ dutasteride in post-menopausal women (contraception if pre-menopausal), correct iron/ thyroid, PRP, LLLT, camouflage, hair transplant"]),
 ("Platelet-rich plasma and other procedures.", ["PRP: autologous platelet concentrate (growth factors — PDGF, VEGF, EGF) injected into scalp monthly × 3 then every 3–6 months; modest evidence", "Microneedling (with minoxidil), low-level laser therapy (650–900 nm) devices, botulinum toxin (limited), mesotherapy; hair transplant — follicular unit transplantation (strip) or follicular unit extraction (FUE); need stable donor area and medical therapy to maintain native hair"]),
 ("Evaluation of a woman with hair loss — what history and tests?", ["Duration, pattern (diffuse vs patterned), shedding vs thinning, menstrual history, hirsutism/ acne, pregnancy, diet/ weight loss, drugs, illness, family history; examine part width, hair pull, trichoscopy", "Tests: CBC, ferritin (aim >40–70 ng/ml), TSH, vitamin D, B12, zinc; androgen profile if signs of hyperandrogenism; scalp biopsy (horizontal sections) when diagnosis unclear"]),
 ("Histopathology of AGA on horizontal sections.", ["Increased vellus-like (miniaturised) hairs with terminal:vellus ratio <4:1 (normal ~7:1), increased telogen, fibrous streamers beneath miniaturised follicles, mild perifollicular lymphocytic inflammation, normal total follicle number (non-scarring)"]),
 ("Drugs causing hair loss (PYQ: effects of drugs on hair).", ["Anagen effluvium: chemotherapy (cyclophosphamide, doxorubicin, taxanes), colchicine, thallium, radiation", "Telogen effluvium: anticoagulants (heparin, warfarin), antithyroid drugs, β-blockers, retinoids (acitretin, isotretinoin), lithium, valproate, carbamazepine, oral contraceptive withdrawal, ACE inhibitors, statins, interferon, antidepressants", "Hypertrichosis/hirsutism: minoxidil, cyclosporine, phenytoin, androgens, steroids, diazoxide, latanoprost (eyelashes), PUVA", "Hair colour changes: chloroquine (lightening), tyrosine kinase inhibitors, cyclosporine/ EGFR inhibitors (curly hair)"]),
 ("What is frontal fibrosing alopecia and why differentiate?", ["Scarring alopecia (LPP variant) in post-menopausal women — band-like recession of frontal/ temporal hairline with perifollicular erythema/ scale, loss of eyebrows, lonely hairs, facial papules; association with sunscreen/ leave-on cosmetics debated", "Differs from AGA (non-scarring, preserved follicular openings); treat with 5-α-reductase inhibitors, HCQ, intralesional steroids, low-dose isotretinoin"]),
 ("AGA and systemic associations.", ["Early-onset AGA in men associated with metabolic syndrome, insulin resistance, coronary heart disease, benign prostatic hyperplasia, hypertension; early 'male PCOS' phenotype; screen cardiovascular risk factors in young men with severe early AGA"]),
 ("Counselling points in AGA treatment.", ["Medical therapy aims to stop progression and partial regrowth; initial shedding with minoxidil in first 2–8 weeks; assessment at 6 and 12 months with standardised photographs; must continue indefinitely; combination therapy better; realistic expectations and camouflage options (fibres, tinted sprays, wigs, scalp micropigmentation)"]),
 ("Differential diagnosis of patterned hair loss.", ["Telogen effluvium, diffuse AA, frontal fibrosing alopecia, LPP, traction alopecia (frontotemporal in tight hairstyles), trichotillomania, hypothyroidism, iron deficiency, secondary syphilis, SLE"]),
 ("What is the 'senescent alopecia'?", ["Age-related diffuse thinning after 60 years without miniaturisation pattern or androgen dependency — uniform reduction of follicles and diameter; may overlap with AGA"]),
 ("What newer drugs are in development for AGA?", ["Topical clascoterone solution (androgen receptor antagonist), pyrilutamide, KX-826, JAK inhibitors (topical — studied), Wnt activators, botulinum toxin, exosomes and stem-cell-derived products (unproven) — check status before quoting"]),
 ("What are the side effects of hair transplant?", ["Folliculitis, cysts, scalp numbness, shock loss of native hair, 'pluggy' appearance (older techniques), donor-site scarring (strip), unnatural hairline, infection, bleeding"]),
 ("What scales measure hair growth in trials?", ["Global photographic assessment, phototrichogram/ trichoscan (hair count and density in a target area — 1 cm²), hair weight, patient self-assessment questionnaires"]),
 ("Why does the occiput not bald in AGA?", ["Occipital follicles have fewer androgen receptors and less 5α-reductase activity — 'donor dominance' (transplanted occipital follicles maintain resistance at recipient site)"]),
 ]),
dict(case="Basal cell carcinoma", qa=[
 ("Explain the molecular pathogenesis.", ["Constitutive activation of Hedgehog signalling: loss-of-function PTCH1 (≈ 70–90%) or activating SMO mutations → GLI transcription factors → cell proliferation; UV-signature TP53 mutations; basis for Hedgehog-pathway inhibitors (vismodegib, sonidegib)"]),
 ("Describe nodular BCC clinically.", ["Commonest (60%): pearly, translucent, dome-shaped papule/nodule with rolled edges and arborising telangiectasia, central ulceration (rodent ulcer) with crust, bleeds on minor trauma; head and neck (nose, inner canthus, nasolabial folds)"]),
 ("Describe superficial, morphoeic and pigmented BCC.", ["Superficial: pink scaly plaque with thread-like raised pearly border on trunk; mimics eczema/ psoriasis/ Bowen's — treatable topically", "Morphoeic (sclerosing/infiltrative): ill-defined, scar-like, ivory/white indurated plaque — aggressive subclinical spread, high recurrence; Mohs preferred", "Pigmented BCC: common in Indian/ Asian skin — brown-black pearly papules with blue-grey globules — mimics melanoma/ seborrhoeic keratosis"]),
 ("What are high-risk features of BCC (NCCN)?", ["Location: H-zone of face (central face, eyelids, eyebrows, nose, lips, chin, ears, temples) any size; ≥20 mm on trunk/ extremities; poorly defined borders; recurrent; immunosuppression; prior radiotherapy site; aggressive histology (morphoeic, infiltrative, micronodular, basosquamous, perineural invasion)"]),
 ("Topical and non-surgical treatments.", ["Imiquimod 5% (5 days/week for 6 weeks) and 5-FU 5% (twice daily 3–6 weeks) — superficial BCC; photodynamic therapy (MAL/ALA) — superficial and thin nodular; cryotherapy (−50 °C double freeze–thaw) — well-defined low-risk lesions; curettage and electrodesiccation (3 cycles) for small low-risk lesions on non-hair-bearing sites; radiotherapy — elderly, inoperable"]),
 ("Surgical margins for BCC.", ["Standard excision with 4 mm clinical margin for well-defined low-risk BCC <2 cm (≈ 95% clearance); larger (5–15 mm) for high-risk or Mohs micrographic surgery (best cure ~99% primary, ~94% recurrent)"]),
 ("Hedgehog inhibitors — indications and side effects.", ["Vismodegib 150 mg/day, sonidegib 200 mg/day — locally advanced BCC not amenable to surgery/RT, metastatic BCC, Gorlin syndrome", "Side effects: muscle spasms, alopecia, dysgeusia (taste loss), weight loss, fatigue, raised CK, teratogenicity; resistance (SMO mutations); cemiplimab (PD-1) for those failing Hh inhibitors"]),
 ("Gorlin syndrome — diagnostic criteria.", ["Major: >2 BCCs or 1 under age 20, odontogenic keratocysts of jaw, ≥3 palmar/plantar pits, bilamellar calcification of falx cerebri, bifid/fused/splayed ribs, first-degree relative with NBCCS, PTCH1/SUFU mutation", "Minor: macrocephaly, frontal bossing, cleft lip/palate, hypertelorism, medulloblastoma (desmoplastic — SUFU), ovarian/cardiac fibromas, lymphomesenteric cysts, polydactyly, Sprengel deformity, vertebral anomalies", "Diagnosis: 2 major or 1 major + 2 minor; avoid radiotherapy (induces many BCCs)"]),
 ("Prognosis and follow-up.", ["Metastasis very rare (<0.1%); local destruction if neglected (rodent ulcer — invasion of bone, orbit, CNS); ~30–50% develop another BCC within 5 years — annual skin examination, sun protection, nicotinamide in high-risk"]),
 ("Why is BCC less common in Indians, and what presentation is typical?", ["Melanin protection against UV; still seen in fair-skinned Indians, elderly, outdoor workers, arsenic exposure, xeroderma pigmentosum, albinism; typically pigmented nodular BCC on face (nose, cheek, eyelid) — often confused with naevus or seborrhoeic keratosis"]),
 ("What is basosquamous carcinoma?", ["Aggressive BCC variant with squamous differentiation — higher local recurrence and metastatic potential (behaves like SCC); treat with Mohs/ wide excision, sentinel node in selected cases"]),
 ("Dermoscopic criteria in more detail.", ["Absence of pigment network plus ≥1 of: arborising vessels, leaf-like areas, large blue-grey ovoid nests, multiple blue-grey globules, spoke-wheel areas, ulceration; superficial BCC — short fine telangiectasia, shiny white-red structureless areas, multiple small erosions"]),
 ("Risk of arsenic-related skin cancer — features.", ["Chronic arsenic (groundwater — West Bengal, Bangladesh; old Fowler's solution): raindrop hyper/ hypopigmentation, punctate palmoplantar keratoses, multiple Bowen's disease on trunk, multiple superficial BCCs and SCCs; internal cancers (lung, bladder)"]),
 ("Reconstruction after BCC excision — principles.", ["Primary closure along relaxed skin tension lines, local flaps (advancement, rotation, transposition — e.g. bilobed for nose), full-thickness skin graft (nasal tip, eyelids), healing by secondary intention for concave areas (inner canthus); ensure clear margins before complex flaps (or delayed reconstruction)"]),
 ("What is fibroepithelioma of Pinkus histology?", ["Thin anastomosing strands of basaloid cells extending from the epidermis into a fibrovascular stroma — fenestrated pattern ('lace-like'); clinically a pink sessile papule on lower back — considered a BCC variant (or trichoblastoma variant)"]),
 ("What is the role of nicotinamide?", ["Oral nicotinamide 500 mg twice daily reduced new non-melanoma skin cancers by ~23% and AKs in high-risk patients (ONTRAC trial) — replenishes cellular ATP for DNA repair, reduces UV immunosuppression; benefit lost on stopping"]),
 ("Differential diagnosis of a pearly nodule on the face.", ["Intradermal naevus, sebaceous hyperplasia, trichoepithelioma (no ulceration, smooth, bluish), adnexal tumours, amelanotic melanoma, keratoacanthoma, Merkel cell carcinoma (violaceous fast-growing), molluscum (umbilicated), cylindroma"]),
 ("Radiotherapy in BCC — indications and contraindications.", ["Indications: elderly patients unfit for surgery, large lesions in difficult sites (eyelid, nose), adjuvant for perineural invasion/ positive margins", "Contraindications: Gorlin syndrome, XP, young patients (late radiation damage, secondary cancers), connective tissue disease (scleroderma), recurrent after RT"]),
 ("What is the histological classification and prognostic relevance?", ["Low-risk: nodular, superficial, pigmented, infundibulocystic, fibroepithelial", "High-risk: micronodular, infiltrative, sclerosing/morphoeic, basosquamous, BCC with sarcomatoid differentiation — need wider margins/ Mohs"]),
 ]),
dict(case="Squamous cell carcinoma", qa=[
 ("Describe precursor lesions of SCC.", ["Actinic keratosis (most common precursor), Bowen's disease (SCC in situ), actinic cheilitis (lower lip), erythroplasia of Queyrat (glans), leukoplakia/ erythroplakia (oral), bowenoid papulosis, arsenical keratoses, chronic radiation dermatitis, chronic scars/ ulcers (Marjolin), porokeratosis, LS, erosive LP, HPV-related warts in immunosuppressed"]),
 ("What is Bowen's disease and its management?", ["Intraepidermal SCC (full-thickness atypia): slowly enlarging, well-demarcated, red scaly plaque on sun-exposed skin (legs of elderly women), or trunk (arsenic); ~3–5% progress to invasive SCC", "Treatment: 5-FU, imiquimod, PDT (excellent for large/leg lesions with poor healing), cryotherapy, curettage, excision; erythroplasia of Queyrat — glans (higher progression)"]),
 ("What is keratoacanthoma?", ["Rapidly growing (weeks) dome-shaped nodule with central keratin-filled crater on sun-exposed skin of elderly; may involute spontaneously in months leaving scar; considered a well-differentiated SCC variant — treat as SCC (excision); multiple KAs — Ferguson-Smith (TGFBR1), Grzybowski (eruptive), Muir–Torre; drug-induced (BRAF inhibitors — vemurafenib)"]),
 ("Describe the TNM staging principles of cSCC (AJCC 8th, head and neck).", ["T1 <2 cm; T2 2–4 cm; T3 >4 cm or minor bone erosion, perineural invasion, or deep invasion (beyond subcutaneous fat or >6 mm); T4 gross bone/skull base invasion", "N staging by node size/number/extranodal extension; M1 distant metastasis", "Brigham and Women's Hospital (BWH) staging uses high-risk factors (≥2 cm, poorly differentiated, perineural invasion of nerve ≥0.1 mm, invasion beyond fat) — better prognostic discrimination"]),
 ("Which areas carry higher metastatic risk?", ["Lip and ear (mucocutaneous junctions), temple, scalp, genitals (penile/vulval), SCC arising in chronic scars/ulcers (Marjolin) or radiation sites, perineural invasion, immunosuppressed patients — metastasis rates 10–30% vs ~2–5% overall"]),
 ("Surgical management and margins.", ["Low-risk SCC: excision with 4–6 mm clinical margins; high-risk: ≥6–10 mm margins or Mohs micrographic surgery (complete margin control); assess regional nodes clinically/ ultrasound; sentinel lymph node biopsy in selected high-risk tumours", "Adjuvant radiotherapy for perineural invasion, close/positive margins, nodal disease with extracapsular extension"]),
 ("Systemic treatment of advanced SCC.", ["Immune checkpoint inhibitors (anti-PD-1) — cemiplimab (first-line for locally advanced/ metastatic cSCC — response ~45–50%), pembrolizumab; cetuximab (EGFR) for those unfit for immunotherapy; platinum chemotherapy older option; transplant recipients — reduce immunosuppression, switch to sirolimus (mTOR inhibitors have anti-tumour effect)"]),
 ("SCC in organ transplant recipients — why and how to manage?", ["Immunosuppression (azathioprine, calcineurin inhibitors) + UV + HPV → SCC 65–250× higher, more aggressive, multiple, SCC:BCC ratio reversed (~4:1)", "Management: strict sun protection, regular skin checks (3–12 monthly), early treatment of AKs (field therapy), nicotinamide, acitretin chemoprevention (10–25 mg/day), reduce/switch immunosuppression (sirolimus/everolimus)"]),
 ("Penile and vulval SCC — risk factors.", ["Penile: phimosis, poor hygiene, uncircumcised, HPV 16/18, lichen sclerosus (BXO), smoking, PUVA; presents as ulcer/ growth on glans/ prepuce; inguinal nodes", "Vulval: HPV-related (younger — usual VIN) and HPV-independent (older — LS/differentiated VIN, p53 mutation); itchy lump/ ulcer"]),
 ("Oral SCC and its premalignant lesions in India.", ["High burden due to tobacco chewing (gutka, khaini), betel quid, smoking, alcohol; premalignant — leukoplakia (white plaque not rubbed off), erythroplakia (highest risk), oral submucous fibrosis (areca nut — trismus, blanching), erosive LP; buccal mucosa commonest site in India; biopsy persistent lesions >2 weeks"]),
 ("Verrucous carcinoma — what is it?", ["Well-differentiated low-grade SCC variant — slow-growing exophytic cauliflower-like mass with 'pushing' borders; sites — oral (oral florid papillomatosis), anogenital (Buschke–Löwenstein), plantar (epithelioma cuniculatum — sinus tracts like rabbit burrows); rarely metastasises; wide excision (radiotherapy may cause anaplastic transformation)"]),
 ("Histological grading (Broders).", ["Grade 1: >75% differentiated (keratin pearls, intercellular bridges); 2: 50–75%; 3: 25–50%; 4: <25% (poorly differentiated, spindle cell)", "Variants: acantholytic, spindle cell (sarcomatoid — cytokeratin+), desmoplastic (high-risk), adenosquamous, clear cell"]),
 ("Differentiate keratoacanthoma from SCC clinically and histologically.", ["KA: rapid growth over weeks, symmetrical crateriform with central keratin plug, may regress; histology — symmetrical cup-shaped architecture with 'lipping' buttresses, glassy eosinophilic keratinocytes, intraepithelial neutrophilic microabscesses", "SCC: slower, irregular, ulcerated, infiltrative strands with atypia; often impossible to separate — treat KA as SCC"]),
 ("Prevention and chemoprevention.", ["Sun protection (sunscreens reduce SCC — Nambour trial), avoid tanning, treat AK, nicotinamide 500 mg twice daily, acitretin in transplant recipients and XP, HPV vaccination (anogenital), stop tobacco/areca nut (oral), avoid arsenic-contaminated water"]),
 ("What is Marjolin's ulcer?", ["SCC arising in chronic scars (burns commonest), chronic osteomyelitis sinuses, venous ulcers, pressure sores, lupus vulgaris, DLE, hidradenitis; long latency (decades); aggressive with high nodal metastasis — biopsy any non-healing ulcer with raised edge/ growth"]),
 ("Examination of a patient with suspected SCC — what do you check?", ["Lesion: size, site, edge (raised/ everted), base, induration, fixation to deeper structures, bleeding, perineural symptoms (pain, numbness, facial weakness)", "Regional lymph nodes (parotid, cervical, axillary, inguinal), whole-skin exam for other cancers/ AKs, oral cavity, immune status"]),
 ("Prognosis.", ["Overall 5-year cure >90% for primary low-risk SCC after excision; recurrent, perineural, poorly differentiated, immunosuppressed and nodal disease have worse outcome (nodal metastasis 5-year survival ~50%)"]),
 ("Cutaneous markers of internal malignancy (expanded).", ["Paraneoplastic dermatoses: acanthosis nigricans (malignant), tripe palms, Leser–Trélat sign, acrokeratosis paraneoplastica of Bazex (upper aerodigestive SCC), erythema gyratum repens (lung), necrolytic migratory erythema (glucagonoma), hypertrichosis lanuginosa acquisita, dermatomyositis, paraneoplastic pemphigus, Sweet syndrome (AML), pyoderma gangrenosum (myeloma), Trousseau sign (migratory thrombophlebitis — pancreatic), carcinoid flushing, Sister Mary Joseph nodule (umbilical metastasis), extramammary Paget's, multicentric reticulohistiocytosis, pachydermoperiostosis/ clubbing (lung)"]),
 ("Bazex syndrome — explain.", ["Acrokeratosis paraneoplastica: psoriasiform violaceous scaly plaques on ears, nose, fingers/ toes and nails (acral) in men >40 with SCC of upper aerodigestive tract (pharynx, larynx, oesophagus) or lung; improves with tumour treatment"]),
 ]),
]
